    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 run / 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `membershipTier` `GOLD` rule now works, implemented by mirroring the existing `namesakeCount`/`CountNamesakes` pattern already in the codebase.\n\nChanges (5 files, each a small cohesive unit):\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `householdCount` field with a plain getter/setter (mirrors `namesakeCount`; the entity's other methods were left untouched).\n2. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `household_count INTEGER` column (only the hsqldb schema carries these evolution columns, matching how `namesake_count` was done).\n3. **`CountHousehold.java`** (new) \u2014 a single-method function that, at create time before save, counts existing owners in the same household (same last name + normalized address, matching the duplicate-household guard and the mapper's `householdId`) and stores `existing + 1` = members after this create.\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `countHousehold` step into the create pipeline (under the transaction governance) between `countNamesakes` and `save`.\n5. **`OwnerMapper.membershipTier`** \u2014 added a leading guard returning `\"GOLD\"` when `householdCount >= 3`; the existing SILVER/BRONZE logic is unchanged and falls through otherwise.\n\nStructural impact was kept low: the counting logic lives in a brand-new tiny class (zero surrounding complexity), the entity change is just a field plus trivial accessors, and the only pre-existing function modified is `membershipTier`, which has minimal surrounding class complexity. No new file grows a large method or class.",
